WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:17.920
SPEAKER: So here is our model of the entire Giza Necropolis.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.400
You'll see we've had to remove modern structures and excavators' debris

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:22.090
dumps.

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:25.540
We studied the Nile, and we had to move it much closer to the Giza pyramids,

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:28.450
because in antiquity, the Nile did flow closer.

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:31.900
And we've tried to rebuild each and every structure.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:34.910
Not all of them are yet in the level of detail that we would like,

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:36.460
but we had most of them in place.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:39.130
The Sphinx is probably the most accurate reconstruction

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:41.080
that has been attempted so far.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:44.280
And you can see the central field mastabas, the Eastern cemetery,

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.960
the West, all of the major components in place.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:51.640
This is now the centralized study tool and research tool.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.957
And as we link all these diverse data, we

00:00:53.957 --> 00:00:56.290
hope that you'll eventually be able to navigate anywhere

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:59.290
you want to go, click on anything that grabs your interest,

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:03.005
and up will pop the related diverse information.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:08.640
That extends to the Sphinx as well.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.580
And what's fascinating about this type of 3D visualization

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.710
is it calls up questions that you may not have

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:16.800
considered in more traditional avenues.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.600
So for example, color on the Sphinx, was the Sphinx entirely painted?

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.250
Was just the head painted?

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.770
We don't have enough surviving exterior blocks

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:29.010
to really come to a secure conclusion about that.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:32.580
So we study what we can, and we have to move forward with the best information

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.140
that we have at the time.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.660
This comparison image of our 3D model shows you

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:39.540
two possibilities above just the head.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.550
And the Nimitz head cloth is painted.

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:44.610
Down below the entire body is painted in the reddish brown

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.440
reserved for male figures.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.090
What do you think?

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:50.310
Another important aspect of visualization

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:53.160
is of course the z-axis, time.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.900
So we can look at Giza when everything is complete.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:57.840
We can look the Giza at the very beginnings

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.730
when the Great Pyramid is just starting to rise.

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:03.360
And in this video snippet, you can see the Quarry

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.470
where a lot of the core blocks are being cut for the Great Pyramid complex.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:11.100
We'll look at some of these in more detail in other sections

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.500
of this module and other modules.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.170
This photo shows the pyramid temple of Khafre,

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:20.430
a builder of the second pyramid in today's rather ruinous state,

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.920
and then the computer model restores it all.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:26.640
This is based on our study of the architectural features of royal pyramid

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.510
complexes from the Old Kingdom.

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.510
And to date, it's the best reconstruction

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.160
that we've been able to produce.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.010
Seated statues of Khafre, he probably adorned the courtyard.

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:38.340
And in the image, you can see the model bark

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.410
being brought into one of the chambers.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:44.580
This allows us to experiment with the size of this bark or boat carrying

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:48.150
a statue of the King and whether it's big enough or small enough to actually

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:51.750
fit between the doors of the temple.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:55.200
Here is a still rendering from the Sphinx area and the Sphinx

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.280
temple in front of it and then adjacent to it, Khafre Valley Temple.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.490
And you'll notice the harbor coming right up close to the entrance

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:04.320
to the temple.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:07.950
Inside Khafre's Valley Temple, there are emplacements,

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:09.960
little slots in the floor that were meant

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:12.240
to hold seated statues of the King.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:14.860
Here again, visualization can help us.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.130
And the other images that you see here, we've

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:19.860
had to play with the size of the Khafre statues,

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:23.610
trying to understand based on the floor emplacements how large

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:27.240
or how small the statue should be or just how big the platform

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.420
or base for each one might be.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:33.990
This video shows a reenactment of the so-called opening of the mouth

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:35.050
ceremony.

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:38.220
That's the reanimation ceremony for the deceased King

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:42.690
meant to magically and ritually bring him back to life for the nether world.

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:46.590
We've placed the ceremony inside Khufu's pyramid temple.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:50.530
And again, interesting questions come up when you do this kind of visualization

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.890
now with avatars or human characters.

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:55.170
So they're ancient Egyptians all over the scene,

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:57.310
but how many should there be?

00:03:57.310 --> 00:03:59.190
What should they be wearing?

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:00.750
Where are the regular Egyptians?

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:04.320
Are they allowed anywhere near this ceremony, or indeed anywhere near Giza

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:05.610
at all?

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:07.500
Is it daytime or nighttime?

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:10.170
Did this ceremony even take place in this temple

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:14.880
or in some other kind of purification tent or temporary location?

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:17.610
We've also modeled the faces on some of these characters

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:21.790
after the ancient Egyptians whose statues were found nearby

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:23.310
in their various tombs.

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:26.850
So we have King Djedefre, who succeeded Khufu.

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:31.440
We have an Ankhaf, a vizier who was instrumental perhaps in Khufu and maybe

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.930
even into Khafre's reign.

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:37.080
And we have Hemiunu, the architect and engineer

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:40.690
who probably oversaw the construction of the Great Pyramid itself.

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:45.390
Another aspect of visualization allows us to dive down a burial shaft.

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:49.347
This time we're going to a plundered burial chamber, so not too much to see.

00:04:49.347 --> 00:04:51.180
But the interesting point here is that we've

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:54.750
chosen to reconstruct the appearance not in the Fourth Dynasty,

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:59.010
not in antiquity, but rather as it looked in 1906, the year

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:03.240
that George Reisner and the excavation team cleared this burial chamber.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:04.960
So it's a plundered room.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:09.000
And first, we'll show some photographs of how it looked in 1906.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:10.980
Again, a plundered room, just some blocks

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:14.070
lying on the floor, some pieces of wood from the coffin,

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:17.310
some bones from the burial, and there is a little depression

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:21.180
in one corner where the original canopic jars would have been located,

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:24.120
holding the internal organs of the deceased.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:26.880
As we go into the computer model now, we see

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:29.910
that we have reconstructed everything in beautiful color

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:36.060
just as it was in Reisner's drawings and black and white photography from 1906.

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:38.130
No one has been down here since that time,

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:41.130
so it's a pretty accurate and interesting rendering.

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:44.490
Again, you see the blocks on the floor, the pieces of wood, the bones.

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:49.440
And in fact, from these bones which we've located in a storeroom at Giza

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:51.810
and from inscriptions in a nearby tomb, we've

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:55.660
actually been able to identify the owner of this mastaba.

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:58.320
She was a woman named Sedit, and she's the mother

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:02.700
of Merib, whose beautifully decorated tomb is right next door and whose

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:08.020
chapel was removed in the 1840s and can now be seen in the Berlin Neues Museum.

00:06:10.734 --> 00:06:13.150
The other wonderful thing about this type of visualization

00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:17.110
is that we can come outside of the chamber into the limestone bedrock

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:21.340
if you will and sort of swim around as no human being can do and get

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:25.570
the underground view of Giza, what I call the "tombs-eye view."

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:28.960
So imagine when all of these hundreds and hundreds of burial shafts

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:33.460
are modeled in such detail as we see here in the case of the burial

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:35.560
chamber of Sedit.

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:36.670
Where is all this going?

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:39.020
Well new technologies are coming up all the time.

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:40.210
Some of them are fantastic.

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:42.670
Some of them are based on older technologies.

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:47.020
You're all familiar with the old stereo view cameras and view devices

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:48.130
from the old days.

00:06:48.130 --> 00:06:51.700
Well that's all many of these 360-degree and panoramic

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:55.000
and stereoscopic headsets are producing for us as well.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:00.020
So we'll carry that further and try to make Giza ever more accessible.

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:02.980
We can even start to recreate the archaeologists themselves.

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:05.290
So one day you may be seeing George Reisner and some

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:11.070
of the others leading you on tours of various areas around Giza themselves.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:18.570
In this example, we're entering a beautifully painted chapel

00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:19.560
of Queen Meresankh III.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:26.690
This was discovered by the Harvard MFA expedition in 1927, of course,

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:29.800
in the last day of work as you'd expect.

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:34.330
Inside were beautifully preserved well paintings of the Queen's chapel.

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:36.880
And tourists can actually visit this tomb today

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:38.920
and see it in all of its glory.

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:42.250
Normally, we couldn't restore all the colors like this without some guidance.

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:45.430
But because the colors remained so beautifully preserved,

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:47.410
we have just about all the information we

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:49.390
need in order to put it back together.

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:56.960
And finally, other experimentation lets us look at individual tomb walls,

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:00.710
click on objects, and have them pop out of the wall in 3D,

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:02.600
so you can rotate them around.

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:04.790
Imagine being able to click on any element--

00:08:04.790 --> 00:08:10.850
a person, a fan, a chest, a box, element of livestock or craftsmanship--

00:08:10.850 --> 00:08:14.029
and have it appear in 3D so that you can study it in more detail.

00:08:14.029 --> 00:08:16.070
These are just some of the experiments that we're

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:20.160
playing with in our visualization attempts with Giza 3D.

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:21.860
Where is all of this going?

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:23.660
Possibly right into your pocket.

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:26.930
It should be possible now to serve edutourism,

00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:29.960
to be able to go to a museum, aim your phone at an object,

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:31.940
and see the original discovery.

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:36.080
Or go out to Giza, aim your phone at a lump of mud or a ruined floor

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:39.679
foundation and see the original temple that once rose there

00:08:39.679 --> 00:08:44.720
or the location of a statue that was once found in that particular location.

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:46.610
In addition, in recent years, colleagues have

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:51.290
been showering the pyramids with cosmic particle rays, muons, in an attempt

00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:56.860
to locate unknown chambers and otherwise hidden cavities.

00:08:56.860 --> 00:08:58.960
We're working on different pathways to allow

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:02.020
you to get into Giza whether you know everything about the site

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:03.250
or nothing about the site.

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:05.380
So there should be something for everyone

00:09:05.380 --> 00:09:08.860
in our efforts to make Giza more accessible to the experts

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:10.750
and the amateurs alike.

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:12.670
And I'll finish with just this comparison,

00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:15.670
because as we produce all this material, we constantly

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:19.780
think what's going to survive, how do we make this sustainable.

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:22.060
Up above you see a typical hard drive.

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:24.130
Down below you see a slab stele.

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:28.710
Which one of these do you think will still be readable in a few years?

